## Barber application checks

Use `examples/barber/README.md` for the Compose and seed commands. Shared HTTP and browser tests must run against both independent backends when changing shared user flows. Preserve tests for login, role scopes, owner/client write boundaries, booking transitions, and MCP authorization. Both backends have real-database checks for transactions, derived booking/bundle/review values, and dataset load/delete behavior. PostgreSQL also checks physical FKs and frontend query selections.

These checks mutate data. Use the isolated example Compose projects or a disposable database; never target a user's existing demo or production database. Keep generated PostgreSQL SDL/SQL/FK exports in ignored `examples/barber/postgres/generated/` and keep credentials, uploads and build output out of Git. See `simfinity-barber-examples.mdc` for the example boundaries.

## Committed controller callbacks

`tests/transaction-callbacks.test.js` checks per-attempt queues; `tests/after-commit-transactions.test.js` checks MongoDB/SQL retries, uncertainty, rollback and cleanup failures; `tests/after-commit.test.js` checks core callbacks, ordering, snapshots, context and error isolation. `tests/integration/after-commit.test.js` exercises MongoDB off/transactional integrity and PostgreSQL with disposable URI fixtures, independent committed reads, legacy rollback, retries, external sessions and a one-client SQL pool. Callback assertions are collected through `configureAfterCommit({ onError })` and checked after each test, so swallowed callback failures cannot hide an assertion failure.
